> This dissertation investigates how environmental risks impact economic and political dynamics. It explores three main areas: voter reactions to natural disasters, the effects of PFAS contamination on infant health, and the allocation of public spending on environmental issues. Chapter 1 examines the influence of natural disasters on U.S. presidential elections. Chapter 2 analyzes how PFAS contamination affects birth outcomes based on environmental conditions. Chapter 3 considers the policy implications, comparing prevention versus remediation in PFAS spending. The overarching question addresses who bears the damage and responsibility for environmental risks, contributing to environmental economics by linking issues of damages, health, and regulation.
